  "query_preview": "..Strong high pressure is centered roughly over Utah\nthis morning, resulting in clear skies just about areawide along\nwith generally light winds. With this ridge strengthening,\ntemperatures will be on the rise, with afternoon highs anticipated\nto be up to 10F above seasonal normals. This means highs of 100F\nor above for all of the valleys, with lows in the 70s to around 80\nfor many locations providing little in the way of overnight\nrelief. Going Heat Advisories and Extreme Heat Warnings cover the\nthreat well. \n\nFor Sunday, the ridge gets nudged eastward by a trough moving\nonshore along the Pacific Northwest coast. This system will track\neast across Idaho and Montana through Monday, with conditions\nstaying dry across the area. The system will have two main impacts\nfor the forecast area: decreasing temperatures and increasing\nwinds. There is high confidence that the trough will drop a dry\ncold front into the area late Sunday into Monday. As a result,\ntemperatures will drop noticeably Monday for northern Utah and\nsouthwest Wyoming, with highs running a few degrees above seasonal\nnormals, something that has seemed rare this summer. \n\nAhead of the front, west to southwest winds will pickup, most \nnoticeably over southwestern and northeastern portions of the \narea. Given the very dry airmass, this will produce areas of \ncritical fire weather conditions. Winds are also expected to \nbecome quite gusty out of the northwest over northwest Utah as the\nfront moves through Sunday night into early Monday. Would not be \nsurprised to see a few gusts in excess of 40 mph during this time,\nbut there is a low chance that the duration will be long enough \nto warrant wind headlines. Also, given the timing of the winds, \nthe effect on fire danger should be more limited. \n\nBehind the trough, a zonal flow will persist into midweek,\ncontinuing to move drier air into the area. Most guidance shows \nthe ridge redeveloping along the Pacific coast by midweek, keeping\na west to northwest flow over the area for a prolonged period. \nGiven the position of the ridge, guidance is indicating at least a\nsmall intrusion of monsoon moisture into southern Utah later in \nthe week, but confidence remains low on the amount of moisture and\nhow far north the moisture will advect. The presence of the ridge\nwill also bring a general warming trend for the latter half of the\nupcoming week, but there remains uncertainty on the extent of the\nwarming.",
